=== L2. Corollary L2: Einstein tensors (n = 4) and the contradiction with J5(b) ===
  [True] (4,0) Einstein, R = W + (Lambda/3) R_1 with W an arbitrary Weyl tensor (10 parameters), |R_1|^2 = 24: C1 = 6|W|^2 + 16 Lambda^2, C2sym = 3/2 |W|^2 + 12 Lambda^2, so 4 C2sym - C1 = 32 Lambda^2 (side: C2 = 3/4 |W|^2 + 16 Lambda^2)
  [True] (1,3) Einstein, R = W + (Lambda/3) R_1 with W an arbitrary Weyl tensor (10 parameters), |R_1|^2 = 24: C1 = 6|W|^2 + 16 Lambda^2, C2sym = 3/2 |W|^2 + 12 Lambda^2, so 4 C2sym - C1 = 32 Lambda^2 (side: C2 = 3/4 |W|^2 + 16 Lambda^2)
     constant curvature +1 (Lambda = 3, W = 0): C1 = 144, C2sym = 108  (nonzero, as in J5(d))
  [True] (3,1) Einstein, R = W + (Lambda/3) R_1 with W an arbitrary Weyl tensor (10 parameters), |R_1|^2 = 24: C1 = 6|W|^2 + 16 Lambda^2, C2sym = 3/2 |W|^2 + 12 Lambda^2, so 4 C2sym - C1 = 32 Lambda^2 (side: C2 = 3/4 |W|^2 + 16 Lambda^2)
  [True] (2,2) Einstein, R = W + (Lambda/3) R_1 with W an arbitrary Weyl tensor (10 parameters), |R_1|^2 = 24: C1 = 6|W|^2 + 16 Lambda^2, C2sym = 3/2 |W|^2 + 12 Lambda^2, so 4 C2sym - C1 = 32 Lambda^2 (side: C2 = 3/4 |W|^2 + 16 Lambda^2)
     constant curvature +1 (Lambda = 3, W = 0): C1 = 144, C2sym = 108  (nonzero, as in J5(d))
  So F(X,Y;h,h) = 0 for all X, Y, h forces Lambda = 0 for every Einstein tensor, every signature; the scale
  of R is irrelevant (F is homogeneous of degree 2 in R), and so is the sign of Lambda.
  [True] J5(b): at x0 = pi(p), Ric(g) = Lambda_g g with Lambda_g = -c_H/c_V, so 4 C2sym - C1 = 8|Ric|^2 = 32 Lambda_g^2 = 32 c_H^2/c_V^2 > 0, whereas the order-eps^2 leaf condition gives C1 = C2sym = 0
  Hence no radial Z2 solution of Upsilon = 0 on a neighbourhood of a point of s(X) in the two settings of J1-J2:
  (14,0) (definite base) and (7,7) (base (1,3) or (3,1), A4; base (2,2), A5); lambda > 1/4 for (7,7).
  The leaf argument itself does not depend on the signature; (8,6), (10,4) etc. lack J1-J2, hence J5(b).

=== L3. Proposition L3: positivity for a Lorentzian (or definite) base, one value of F ===
  [True] (4,0), T = e_0 (g(T,T) = 1): F(T,T; T^b (x) T^b) = 2 g(T,T) sum_ij eta_i eta_j R(T,e_i,T,e_j)^2 over T^perp, generic R (20 components); T^perp definite: True
  [True] (1,3), T = e_0 (g(T,T) = 1): F(T,T; T^b (x) T^b) = 2 g(T,T) sum_ij eta_i eta_j R(T,e_i,T,e_j)^2 over T^perp, generic R (20 components); T^perp definite: True
  [True] (3,1), T = e_3 (g(T,T) = -1): F(T,T; T^b (x) T^b) = 2 g(T,T) sum_ij eta_i eta_j R(T,e_i,T,e_j)^2 over T^perp, generic R (20 components); T^perp definite: True
  [True] (2,2), T = e_0 (g(T,T) = 1): F(T,T; T^b (x) T^b) = 2 g(T,T) sum_ij eta_i eta_j R(T,e_i,T,e_j)^2 over T^perp, generic R (20 components); T^perp definite: False
  [True] R(T,X,T,Y) := g(R(T,X)T, Y) as a polynomial in (T, X, Y) determines R: the linear map from the 20 components has rank 20 (so its vanishing for T in the open timelike cone forces R = 0)
  So for a base of signature (1,3), (3,1) or (4,0): F(X,X;h,h) = 0 for all X, h forces R = 0, for every
  algebraic curvature tensor (Einstein or not).  For (2,2), T^perp is indefinite and no such value is definite (L4).

=== L4. Proposition L4: the coefficients of the quadratic form F(X,X;h,h) (n = 4, Einstein); base (2,2) ===
  [True] (1,3): the 532 coefficients of F(X,X;h) (X in R^4, h in Sym^2) span a space of quadratic forms in (W, Lambda) of dimension 47 (of 66), and Lambda^2 lies in it  [110 s]
  [True] (2,2): the 532 coefficients of F(X,X;h) (X in R^4, h in Sym^2) span a space of quadratic forms in (W, Lambda) of dimension 47 (of 66), and Lambda^2 lies in it  [123 s]
  [True] (4,0): the 532 coefficients of F(X,X;h) (X in R^4, h in Sym^2) span a space of quadratic forms in (W, Lambda) of dimension 47 (of 66), and Lambda^2 lies in it  [135 s]
  (3,1) and (0,4) follow from (1,3) and (4,0) by g -> -g, which leaves the endomorphisms R(X,Y) and G_0
  unchanged and sends F -> -F (and Lambda -> -Lambda): same span, same rank.
  [True] (2,2): *^2 = 1, [R, *] = 0 for the Einstein curvature operator, R = R+ (+) R- on Lambda+ (+) Lambda-, induced forms [[-2, 0, 0], [0, -2, 0], [0, 0, 2]] and [[-2, 0, 0], [0, -2, 0], [0, 0, 2]] (signature (1,2) each), tr R+ = tr R- = Lambda (so R+- = W+- + Lambda/3)
  [True] (2,2): all 81 products (R+)_ij (R-)_ab lie in the span of the coefficients of F (they span 36 of its 47 dimensions); hence F = 0 forces R+ = 0 or R- = 0
  [True] (2,2), on R+ = 0: Lambda = 0 there, 5 parameters remain (the other half W); the span of the coefficients of F equals the span of the entries of (the other block)^2 (both of dimension 6)
  [True] (2,2), on R- = 0: Lambda = 0 there, 5 parameters remain (the other half W); the span of the coefficients of F equals the span of the entries of (the other block)^2 (both of dimension 6)
  So in base (2,2) the Einstein solutions of F = 0 are exactly: Lambda = 0, one of W+, W- zero and the other
  self-adjoint with square 0, i.e. R = c n (x) n with n a null element of Lambda+ or Lambda- (c real).
  [True] (2,2) example: n = [-1, 0, 1, 1, 0, 1] (components on e^a^e^b, a<b) is anti-self-dual and null; R = n (x) n satisfies Bianchi, Ric = 0, n o n = 0, F = 0, and the full leaf condition det(y)^2 Phi_y(X,Y) = 0 identically in the symmetric y and in X, Y
  (By hand: R(X,e_i) = n(X,e_i) N with N the endomorphism of n, so every term of Phi_y carries the factor
   sum_ij g^ij n(X,e_i) n(Y,e_j) = (n o n)(X,Y) = 0.)  These Ricci-flat tensors pass the HH leaf condition to
   all orders; they are excluded in J5 only because there Lambda_g = -c_H/c_V != 0.

